  ── Distribution Shape ──────────────────────
  Why: Skewness and kurtosis indicate departures from a normal (bell-shaped) distribution.
  Skewness            1.6226		Substantial right skew (|skew| < 2 is typically acceptable)
  Kurtosis (excess)   4.1827		Near-normal peakedness (0 = normal; |excess| < 7 treated as typical)
  → Skewness within typical limits (criterion: |skew| < 2)
  → Kurtosis within typical limits (criterion: |excess kurt| < 7)


  ── Shapiro-Wilk Test ───────────────────────
  Why: Formal test of whether the data are drawn from a normal distribution.
  W                   0.8827		Closer to 1 = more normal (range: 0 to 1)
  p                   < .001  (3.27e-05)		Statistically significant: if there were truly no effect, results at least this extreme would occur less than 0.1% of the time
  → Rejects normality (p < 0.05)


  ── Recommendation ──────────────────────────
  Shapiro-Wilk rejects normality (p < .05), but skewness and kurtosis are
  within the thresholds and n = 60. At this sample size the test
  detects departures too small to unsettle a parametric procedure.
  → Parametric tests (t-test, ANOVA, Pearson r) are recommended.
  → Nonparametric tests (Mann-Whitney, Kruskal-Wallis, Spearman rho) remain
     a defensible choice, since the test did reject.
  What was checked: the marginal distribution of this column. Parametric
  tests assume normality of the model RESIDUALS, which is not the same
  distribution and is not examined here.

  Conventions: quartiles R type 7 · SD & variance n-1
  · rank tests average tied ranks (tie-corrected).
